#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# discrete_lyapunov_solve

#% text
# `discrete_lyapunov_solve(a, c)` gives the matrix x with `a . x . conjugate_transpose(a) - x == c`.

#% text
# `discrete_lyapunov_solve(a, b, c)` gives the matrix x with `a . x . b - x == c`; `discrete_lyapunov_solve([a, d], [b, e], c)` the one with `a . x . b - d . x . e == c`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The equation is solved as a linear system with Kronecker products; exact matrices give exact solutions.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
discrete_lyapunov_solve([[3, 2], [1, 1]], [[0, 2], [1, 2]], [[2, 2], [0, 1]])
#% output
#= [[-6/11, 2/11], [7/11, 5/11]]

#% cell
discrete_lyapunov_solve([[-0.5, 1], [-0.1, -0.4]], [[1, 0], [1, -1]])
#% output
#= [[1.4480519480519485, -0.25649350649350655], [-1.6850649350649354, 1.1152597402597404]]

#% cell
discrete_lyapunov_solve([[-1/2, 0], [0, 1/2]], [[1, 0], [0, 1]])
#% output
#= [[-4/3, 0], [0, -4/3]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `lyapunov_solve`, `discrete_riccati_solve`
